+# expect_all_false
+
+::: {.callout-note title="Added in 0.7.0" .low-opacity}
+:::
+
+## What it does
+
+Checks for usage of `expect_true(all(!x))`.
+
+## Why is this bad?
+
+`expect_all_false()` states the intent more directly than
+`expect_true(all(!x))`, and when the test fails it reports which elements
+are not `FALSE` instead of only saying that `all(!x)` is not `TRUE`.
+
+`expect_all_false()` was added in testthat 3.3.0.
+
+This rule is **disabled by default**. Select it either with the rule name
+`"expect_all_false"` or with the rule group `"TESTTHAT"`.
+
+This rule doesn't report calls that pass `info` or `label` to
+`expect_true()`, or that pass several arguments (such as `na.rm`) to
+`all()`.
+
+This rule has an automatic fix that is marked unsafe and therefore requires
+passing `--unsafe-fixes`. `expect_all_false()` is stricter than
+`expect_true(all(!x))`: it errors when `x` is empty and can fail when `x`
+isn't a logical vector (for example, `x <- c(0, 0)`), so a test that passes
+now could fail after the fix.
+
+## Example
+
+```r
+expect_true(all(!x))
+expect_true(all(!is.na(x)))
+```
+
+Use instead:
+```r
+expect_all_false(x)
+expect_all_false(is.na(x))
+```
+
+## References
+
+See `?testthat::expect_all_false`
-- 
